    slug: "informed-consent-in-aesthetic-medicine",
    title: "Principles of Informed Consent in Aesthetic Medicine",
    excerpt: "A guide to informed consent in aesthetic medicine for professionals, covering risks, alternatives, realistic expectations, documentation and scope of practice in Ontario.",
    author: AUTHOR,
    authorTitle: AUTHOR_TITLE,
    authorBio: AUTHOR_BIO,
    publishDate: "2026-09-27",
    category: "Patient Communication",
    bodyHtml: `
<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Informed consent is a cornerstone of ethical and safe aesthetic medicine. It is far more than a signature on a form; it is a process of genuine communication that respects patient autonomy and supports good decision-making. For dental and healthcare professionals offering aesthetic procedures within their scope, mastering consent is as important as mastering technique. This article outlines the core principles of informed consent in aesthetic medicine, including risks, alternatives, realistic expectations and documentation, with attention to the Ontario regulatory context. For a related perspective, see our article on <a href="/articles/managing-patient-expectations-cosmetic-results">managing patient expectations for cosmetic results</a>.</p><h2>Consent as a Process, Not a Form</h2><p>Valid informed consent rests on several elements: the patient must have the capacity to decide, the decision must be voluntary and free of coercion, and it must be based on adequate information disclosed in understandable terms. In aesthetics, where procedures are typically elective, this standard is especially important. Patients are choosing treatment they do not medically require, so their understanding and genuine agreement are paramount.</p><p>Consent should be obtained before the procedure, with enough time for the patient to ask questions and reflect. Professional bodies such as the Canadian Dental Association and the College of Nurses of Ontario emphasize that consent is an ongoing dialogue, and that patients may withdraw consent at any time.</p><h2>Disclosing Risks and Alternatives</h2><p>A central duty is to disclose material risks, those a reasonable patient would want to know, including common side effects and serious but less frequent complications. For injectable procedures this may include bruising, swelling, asymmetry and rarer vascular events. Patients should also understand the alternatives, including less invasive options and the option of no treatment at all.</p><p>Disclosure should be honest and balanced, avoiding any suggestion of guaranteed outcomes. Overstating benefits or minimizing risks undermines valid consent and erodes trust.</p><h3>Setting Realistic Expectations</h3><p>Aesthetic outcomes are influenced by individual anatomy, healing and product behaviour. Part of informed consent is helping patients form realistic expectations about what a procedure can and cannot achieve, how long results may last, and what maintenance might involve. Using clear language, and documenting these conversations, protects both patient and practitioner. For more on this, our article on <a href="/articles/managing-patient-expectations-cosmetic-results">managing patient expectations</a> explores the topic in depth.</p><h2>Documentation and Record-Keeping</h2><p>Thorough documentation is essential. Records should reflect the information provided, the risks and alternatives discussed, the patient's questions and the agreement reached. A signed consent form is useful evidence, but it supports, rather than replaces, the underlying conversation. Good records demonstrate that a proper consent process occurred and support continuity of care.</p><p>Where appropriate, documentation may also include the product used, the areas treated and any pre- and post-procedure instructions given to the patient. Clear records help manage follow-up, inform any future treatment and provide an accurate account should a question or complication arise later. Consistent, contemporaneous note-keeping is a hallmark of professional practice.</p><h2>Consent Within Your Scope of Practice</h2><p>Consent is meaningful only when the procedure is one you are permitted to perform. Scope of practice varies by profession and province, and a training certificate is not a licence or permission to practise. In Ontario, the Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario (RCDSO) specifies that dentists may use botulinum toxin and dermal fillers only within the scope of dentistry, meaning intra-oral therapeutic or cosmetic use, and botulinum toxin extra-orally only for therapeutic purposes such as certain temporomandibular disorder management, not for extra-oral cosmetic purposes. Nurses and physicians are governed by the College of Nurses of Ontario and the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario. Every professional should confirm their permitted scope with their own regulator before offering any procedure.</p><h2>Building Consent Skills Through Training</h2><p>Consultation and consent are learnable skills that quality training should address alongside clinical technique. A well-designed injectable program for medical professionals should cover patient consultation, contraindication assessment and the consent conversation, not just the mechanics of injection. Those interested can <a href="https://www.aesthetictraining.ca/courses/botox-filler" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow sponsored">see how an injectable training course covers consultation and consent</a> as part of its broader curriculum. Pair any such training with careful attention to the consent principles described above and with confirmation of your own scope of practice.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Informed consent in aesthetic medicine is a genuine process of communication: disclosing risks and alternatives, setting realistic expectations, respecting autonomy and documenting thoroughly. For professionals in Ontario, consent is meaningful only within an authorized scope of practice, so verifying that scope with your regulator is a prerequisite, not an afterthought. Strong consent practices protect patients, support trust and reflect the professionalism that aesthetic care demands.</p><h2>References</h2><ol><li>Canadian Dental Association. Principles of informed consent and patient communication. cda-adc.ca.</li><li>College of Nurses of Ontario. Consent Practice Standard. cno.org.</li><li>Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario. Guidance on botulinum toxin and dermal fillers within the scope of dentistry. rcdso.org.</li><li>College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario. Consent to Treatment policy. cpso.on.ca.</li><li>Government of Canada. Health Canada: patient safety and cosmetic procedure information. canada.ca.</li></ol>
`,
