    bodyHtml: `
<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Facial aesthetics has become an area of strong interest for clinicians across several disciplines, including dentists, registered nurses, and physicians. With that interest comes an important responsibility: understanding where your professional boundaries lie. A common misconception is that finishing a hands-on course automatically grants the authority to perform a procedure. In Canada, that is not how regulation works. Training builds competence, but your legal scope of practice is determined by your regulatory college, not by a course certificate. This article outlines how scope is defined across disciplines in Ontario and why boundary awareness is central to responsible practice.</p><h2>What Scope of Practice Actually Means</h2><p>Scope of practice describes the activities a regulated health professional is authorized to perform, based on their education, their college's standards, and the governing legislation. In Ontario, the Regulated Health Professions Act and profession-specific acts set the framework. Each regulatory college then publishes standards and guidelines that interpret and apply that legislation. Importantly, scope is not uniform across professions, and it can differ even among individuals within the same profession depending on their training and the setting in which they work.</p><p>Because of this, two clinicians who complete the same course may have different authority to apply what they learned. A certificate of completion documents that you attended and participated in education. It is not a licence, and it does not override your college's standards. This distinction matters enormously in facial aesthetics, where procedures can carry real clinical risk.</p><h3>Dentists and the RCDSO</h3><p>For dentists in Ontario, the Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario (RCDSO) sets the boundaries. Dentists may use botulinum toxin and dermal fillers only within the scope of dentistry. In practical terms, this means intra-oral therapeutic or cosmetic applications, or botulinum toxin used extra-orally for therapeutic purposes such as certain temporomandibular disorder or orofacial pain conditions. Ontario dentists are not authorized to inject extra-orally for purely cosmetic purposes. Any dentist considering aesthetic training should read the RCDSO's position directly and confirm how it applies to their intended procedures.</p><h3>Nurses and the CNO</h3><p>Registered nurses and nurse practitioners are governed by the College of Nurses of Ontario (CNO). The CNO sets out decision frameworks for whether a nurse may perform a controlled act, often requiring an appropriate order and the right competencies and supports. A nurse's authority to provide injectables is not identical to a dentist's or a physician's, and it depends on CNO standards rather than on any course.</p><h3>Physicians and the CPSO</h3><p>Physicians answer to the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario (CPSO), which maintains its own expectations for cosmetic and aesthetic procedures, delegation, and supervision. Physicians generally hold broader authority for extra-oral cosmetic injectables, but they too must practise within CPSO standards and their own competence.</p><h2>Why Training Does Not Equal Authorization</h2><p>Education and authorization are two different things. A well-structured program can teach anatomy, pharmacology, injection technique, consultation skills, and complication management. What it cannot do is change the law or your college's standards. This is why reputable training organizations are careful to frame their programs as continuing education rather than as permission to practise. If you are weighing your options, it is reasonable to <a href="https://www.aesthetictraining.ca/courses" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow sponsored">browse the full range of continuing-education programs</a> to understand what each one covers, while separately confirming with your regulator what you are actually permitted to perform.</p><p>Approaching education this way protects your patients and your registration. It also sets a professional tone: you are building skill deliberately and staying within your lane. For related reading on how clinicians evaluate the content and quality of these programs, see our overview on <a href="/articles/how-to-evaluate-a-botox-filler-curriculum">how to evaluate a Botox and filler curriculum</a>, and our discussion of a <a href="/articles/safety-first-culture-in-aesthetics-education">safety-first culture in aesthetics education</a>.</p><h2>Practical Steps for Staying Within Scope</h2><p>Start by reading your college's current standards and position statements rather than relying on word of mouth. Document your training and the reasoning behind any new procedure you adopt. When uncertain, contact your college's practice advisory service before offering a service. Finally, revisit these boundaries periodically, because standards evolve. Scope awareness is not a one-time checkbox; it is an ongoing professional habit.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Facial aesthetics can be a meaningful extension of a clinical career, but only when pursued within clearly understood professional boundaries. Training sharpens your skills; your regulatory college defines your authority. Dentists, nurses, and physicians each operate under different standards, and eligibility varies from person to person. Before applying any new skill, verify your scope with your own regulator. That discipline is what separates responsible practice from avoidable risk.</p><h2>References</h2><ol><li>Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario (RCDSO) — Standards of Practice and guidance on botulinum toxin and dermal fillers.</li><li>College of Nurses of Ontario (CNO) — Decisions About Procedures and Authority practice standard.</li><li>College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario (CPSO) — policies on cosmetic and aesthetic procedures.</li><li>Government of Ontario — Regulated Health Professions Act, 1991.</li></ol>
`,
