    bodyHtml: `
<h2>Introduction</h2><p>One of the most common questions among clinicians interested in facial aesthetics is a deceptively simple one: who is actually allowed to take Botox and filler training, and who can apply it clinically afterward? In Ontario, the answer hinges on regulatory scope rather than on the training itself. Eligibility to enrol is one matter; eligibility to perform procedures on patients is another, and it is determined by your regulatory college. This article makes that regulatory nuance the backbone of the discussion, because misunderstanding it is a genuine professional risk.</p><h2>Eligibility to Enrol Versus Eligibility to Practise</h2><p>Reputable injectable training programs limit enrolment to medical professionals who work within a relevant scope of practice. That typically includes dentists, physicians, and nurses, among others. But enrolling in a course is not the same as being authorized to perform the procedures it teaches. Completing a training certificate does not by itself authorize practice. The certificate documents your education; your legal authority comes from your regulatory college. You can see how this is framed in <a href="https://www.aesthetictraining.ca/courses/botox-filler" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow sponsored">the course's published eligibility requirements</a>, which state that eligibility is limited to medical professionals working within their scope.</p><h3>Dentists and the RCDSO Position</h3><p>For dentists in Ontario, the Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario (RCDSO) defines the boundaries, and the nuance here is significant. Dentists may use botulinum toxin and dermal fillers only within the scope of dentistry. This includes intra-oral therapeutic or cosmetic applications. It also includes botulinum toxin used extra-orally for therapeutic purposes, such as managing certain temporomandibular disorder or orofacial pain conditions. However, Ontario dentists are not authorized to inject extra-orally for purely cosmetic purposes. This is a critical distinction: a dentist who completes a general Botox and filler course cannot assume they may offer extra-oral cosmetic injections. They must practise within the RCDSO's defined scope. Any dentist considering this training should read the RCDSO's standards directly and confirm how they apply.</p><h3>Nurses and the CNO</h3><p>Registered nurses and nurse practitioners are governed by the College of Nurses of Ontario (CNO). The CNO uses decision-making frameworks to determine whether a nurse may perform a controlled act, which can involve requirements such as an appropriate order, the necessary competencies, and adequate supports. A nurse's authority is distinct from a dentist's or a physician's and depends entirely on CNO standards.</p><h3>Physicians and the CPSO</h3><p>Physicians are regulated by the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario (CPSO). Physicians generally hold broader authority for extra-oral cosmetic injectables, but they remain bound by CPSO standards, their own competence, and expectations around delegation and supervision where relevant.</p><h2>Why This Nuance Matters</h2><p>The gap between completing training and being authorized to practise is where clinicians most often get into trouble. A certificate can create a false sense of entitlement to perform any procedure the course covered. In reality, scope varies by profession and even by individual, depending on training and setting. Treating education as if it were a licence can put both patients and your registration at risk. Our related article on <a href="/articles/professional-boundaries-scope-of-practice">professional boundaries and scope of practice</a> examines this theme across disciplines, and our guide on <a href="/articles/how-to-evaluate-a-botox-filler-curriculum">how to evaluate a Botox and filler curriculum</a> can help you choose responsible education.</p><h3>How to Confirm Your Own Eligibility</h3><p>Start by reading your regulatory college's current standards and position statements. If anything is unclear, contact your college's practice advisory service before offering a service. Document your training and your reasoning for adopting any new procedure. Because standards evolve, revisit your scope periodically rather than assuming it is fixed.</p><h3>Common Misunderstandings Worth Avoiding</h3><p>Several assumptions trip up well-intentioned clinicians. The first is believing that because a course accepts a particular profession, every member of that profession may perform everything taught. In reality, authority depends on the individual's competence, training, and setting, as well as the college's standards. The second is treating a certificate as portable proof of authority across jurisdictions, when scope is defined provincially and by profession. The third, and most consequential for dentists, is assuming that general injectable training clears the way for extra-oral cosmetic work. It does not; the RCDSO's scope boundary remains in force regardless of what any course covers. Recognizing these pitfalls in advance helps clinicians plan their continuing education around what they will actually be permitted to do.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>In Ontario, who can take Botox and filler training is a narrower question than who can apply it clinically. Enrolment is generally limited to medical professionals working within their scope, but a certificate never substitutes for regulatory authority. Dentists face specific RCDSO limits, including no extra-oral cosmetic injections, while nurses and physicians operate under their own colleges. Before applying any new skill, verify your scope with your own regulator. That single step protects your patients, your practice, and your professional standing.</p><h2>References</h2><ol><li>Royal College of Dental Surgeons of Ontario (RCDSO) — standards on botulinum toxin and dermal fillers within the scope of dentistry.</li><li>College of Nurses of Ontario (CNO) — Decisions About Procedures and Authority practice standard.</li><li>College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario (CPSO) — policies on cosmetic and aesthetic procedures.</li><li>Government of Ontario — Regulated Health Professions Act, 1991.</li></ol>
`,
